  "evidence_supported_observations": [
    "The canonical selective-freeze candidate does not generalize to the hard FD003_TO_FD001 transfer direction: its 3-seed mean is slightly worse than original SSL and clearly worse than the plain no-spec baseline.",
    "Selective freezing still changes the hard-task dynamics materially rather than acting as a no-op: on all three seeds it lowers the best-epoch invariant-MMD loss relative to original SSL.",
    "Lower invariant-MMD alone is not sufficient for better hard-task transfer: seed 44 reduces best-epoch inv-MMD from 0.4365 to 0.3732 under freezing but the test RMSE worsens from 22.4487 to 23.0168.",
    "The hard-task best case is seed 43, where selective freezing simultaneously raises gate activity, raises pseudo acceptance, lowers invariant-MMD, and improves test RMSE from 19.4655 to 18.7522.",
    "On the hard-task bad seed 44, neither lower-LR full adaptation nor lighter transformer/head-only adaptation rescues the SSL branch; both remain worse than the original SSL full run."
  ],
  "evidence_supported_conclusions": [
    "Selective adaptation freezing should not be promoted as a new universal SSL default after the current evidence.",
    "The adaptive-scope hypothesis is still real, but its useful regime is task-conditional rather than universal.",
    "For FD003_TO_FD001 seed 44, the current rescue attempts show that milder updates and stronger freezing both fail, so this hard-task failure is not explained by a simple 'update too aggressively' story."
  ],
  "open_interpretation": [
    "The hard task may require a different decision rule for when to preserve or update the shared invariant core, rather than a single freeze policy.",
    "A next useful diagnostic would be to compare baseline vs SSL vs selective-freeze early-epoch feature statistics on FD003_TO_FD001 seed 43 and 44 under the same evaluation tap."
  ]
